"""Plain-language labels shared by forms and calculation explanations."""
FIELDS = {
"cash_flow": ("Annual cash available to the business", "Find the annual cash-flow statement and income statement. This is calculated, not usually a printed total: operating profit × (1 − tax rate) + depreciation − spending on equipment − increase in working capital. It is called free cash flow to the firm (FCFF). Do not substitute net profit or cash from operations alone."),
"growth": ("Expected yearly cash growth", "An assumption for the next five years, not a reported figure. 3% is an illustrative starting point; compare several years of recurring cash flows and the company's outlook. Higher growth raises the estimate."),
"discount_rate": ("Required yearly return", "The return used to translate future money into today's value. For business cash flow, use the weighted cost of debt and shareholder funding (WACC). For dividends or shareholder cash flow, use shareholders' required return. The calculator below shows how a starting estimate is built."),
"terminal_growth": ("Growth after year 5", "A long-run assumption used after the five-year forecast. 2% is an illustrative conservative starting point, not company guidance. It must be lower than Required yearly return. Small changes can greatly affect value."),
"net_debt": ("Debt minus available cash", "Calculate interest-bearing borrowings minus excess/available cash, plus any other claims owed ahead of common shareholders. Find borrowings and cash in the balance sheet and notes. Total liabilities is not the same as debt. Do not subtract cash the business needs to operate without reviewing it."),
"eps": ("Earnings per share (EPS)", "Profit attributable to common shareholders divided by the weighted-average common shares. PSE EDGE shows basic and diluted EPS. Use annual or trailing-12-month EPS, not one quarter's EPS. Current P/E = Share price ÷ Earnings per share."),
"price": ("Share price", "Last saved market quote or your manual reference. Refresh online to request the latest available PSE EDGE quote; it may be delayed. Offline calculations use the saved value."),
"multiple": ("Comparison ratio", "A valuation benchmark, separate from the company's current ratio. For P/E, compare annual EPS with suitable companies' P/E ratios or the company's history. Using the current P/E simply reproduces the current share price. A saved benchmark is an assumption you can change, not a guaranteed fair ratio."),
"bvps": ("Book value per share", "Common shareholders' accounting equity divided by common shares. PSE EDGE usually displays it. Current P/B = Share price ÷ Book value per share. Accounting book value is not necessarily market value."),
"shares": ("Number of common shares", "Outstanding common shares, available on the PSE EDGE stock-data page or in the share-capital note. Enter the full count, without converting to millions. Use the appropriate diluted count if your valuation includes dilution."),
"asset_value": ("Value of owned assets and businesses", "For a holding company such as AC, add the value of each business multiplied by the ownership percentage. This is a sum-of-parts estimate, not the consolidated Total Assets line. Subsidiary equity values already reflect their own debt. Use the investment/subsidiary notes and a component-by-component calculation."),
"liabilities": ("Remaining parent-company debt and claims", "For sum-of-parts, subtract parent-company obligations not already deducted from business values. Check the parent-only balance sheet and borrowing notes. Do not subtract the group's consolidated liabilities again. This requires a reviewed calculation; the app cannot infer it from Total Liabilities."),
"discount": ("Discount to asset-based value", "A chosen reduction for holding-company costs, complexity or uncertainty. 0% means no discount. A 15% starting discount is illustrative, not a required accounting adjustment or industry average. Compare 0%, 15% and 25% and explain your choice."),
"dividend": ("Annual dividend per share", "Recurring cash dividends per common share over a year. Find declarations on PSE EDGE's Dividends and Rights page. Exclude one-off special dividends when projecting a permanent payout."),
}
METHODS = {
"pe": "Earnings valuation asks what one share could be worth if investors paid a chosen comparison P/E for its annual earnings. Fair value = Earnings per share × Comparison P/E. It is useful for profitable businesses with reasonably stable earnings. Current P/E measures today's market pricing; it is not an independent fair-value benchmark.",
"pb": "Book-value valuation compares share price with the accounting equity behind each share. Fair value = Book value per share × Comparison P/B. It can be useful for banks and other asset-based businesses, but asset quality and profitability matter. A low current P/B alone does not prove undervaluation.",
"dcf_fcff": "Cash-flow valuation estimates five years of cash the business can generate, adds its continuing value, and converts that future cash into today's money. Subtract Debt minus available cash, then divide by Number of common shares. It is useful when operating cash generation can be forecast. FCFF means business cash before financing; WACC means the blended return required by lenders and shareholders.",
"dcf_fcfe": "Shareholder cash-flow valuation discounts cash remaining for common shareholders after financing. Use shareholders' required return. Debt is already reflected in these cash flows, so do not subtract it again. FCFE means free cash flow to equity.",
"ddm": "Dividend valuation estimates a share's value from sustainable future dividends. Fair value = Annual dividend per share × (1 + Long-run dividend growth) ÷ (Required yearly return − Long-run dividend growth). It fits stable dividend payers. It is less useful when payouts are irregular or not supported by earnings and cash.",
"sotp": "Sum-of-parts values a holding company by adding its ownership share of each business, subtracting remaining parent obligations and applying any chosen holding-company discount. It is useful for companies such as AC whose businesses have different economics. The required business values are estimates, not one figure copied from consolidated Total Assets.",
"nav": "Net asset value estimates what belongs to common shareholders after subtracting obligations from reviewed asset values. Divide by shares, with any chosen discount. It suits asset-based businesses. Market-value NAV and accounting book value are different, so label the basis you use.",
"rnav": "Revalued net asset value uses estimated current values for properties and development projects, less obligations. It suits property businesses. It needs project/property valuations; the balance sheet's historical asset costs are not automatically current market values.",
"dividend_cap": "Dividend capitalization divides a sustainable annual dividend by the yield you would require. For example, PHP 3 at a 6% required yield implies PHP 50. It can help compare income-producing stocks such as REITs. The required yield is a risk judgment, not the current dividend yield copied back into the formula.",
}
RATE_HELP = {
"risk_free": "The saved 91-day Philippine Treasury-bill yield is a short-term project-policy proxy, not a duration-matched rate for a long-lived business. For long-term DCF, consider a dated PHP long-term government benchmark with any appropriate sovereign-risk adjustment. Keep currency, inflation basis and equity premium consistent. Record the source and date in your analysis notes; online stock refresh does not refresh this rate.",
"beta": "How sensitive the stock has historically been to market moves. 1 means roughly market-like sensitivity; above 1 means more; below 1 means less. The default 1 is an illustrative neutral assumption, not an estimated beta for this stock.",
"premium": "Extra return shareholders require above the government rate for equity risk. The starting 6% is an illustrative assumption, not a current official rate. Shareholders' required return = government rate + beta × this premium.",
"debt_cost": "Annual borrowing cost before tax. Estimate interest expense ÷ average interest-bearing debt, or use disclosed loan rates. The starting 7% is illustrative. Find interest expense and borrowing notes in the full annual report.",
"tax": "Tax rate applied to the interest deduction. The starting 25% is illustrative; actual company tax treatment, incentives and deductible interest may differ. Use the tax note to choose an appropriate rate.",
"debt_weight": "Debt as a share of debt plus equity funding: interest-bearing debt ÷ (interest-bearing debt + market value of equity). Market equity = Share price × Number of common shares. The starting 30% is illustrative unless calculated from reviewed inputs.",
}
